## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 0 High · 1 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite is dominated by **self-referential / tautological assertions**. Five of eight tests assert that the output of an operation equals its input, using inputs specifically chosen (or structured) so the operation is a no-op. These tests will pass even if the production code is completely broken for real-world inputs.

---

### Critical Findings

#### 1. `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72) — Tautological assertion

The input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `.ToUpper()` is a no-op, so `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is trivially true. This test can never detect a broken `ToUpper`.

```csharp
// Before (always passes — input is already uppercase)
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// Fix: use an input that actually requires transformation
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

#### 2. `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37) — Tautological assertion

`Normalize` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. The input `"alice"` is already trimmed and lowercase — the operation is a no-op. The test never verifies that normalization actually transforms anything.

```csharp
// Before (input is already normalized — assertion is trivially true)
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// Fix: use inputs that exercise the actual transformation
[DataRow("  Alice  ", "alice")]
[DataRow("BOB", "bob")]
[DataRow(" Charlie ", "charlie")]
[TestMethod]
public void NormalizeName_TrimsAndLowercases(string input, string expected)
{
    var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}
```

---

#### 3. `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56) — Self-referential assertion

The test asserts `user.Name == display`, but `GetDisplayName()` just returns `Name`. This is asserting a property against itself. The test name says "FormattedName" but no formatting occurs — if formatting were added later, this test would *break* rather than validate it.

```csharp
// Before (asserts output == the same property used to produce it)
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// Fix: assert against a literal expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName should format (e.g., "Alice (30)"), assert that instead
```

---

#### 4. `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 64) — Self-referential + missing negative test

`ValidateEmail` returns the input on success. Asserting `input == result` is trivially true for any valid input. More critically, there is **no test that invalid input throws** — the only interesting behavior of a validator.

```csharp
// Before (trivially true for valid input)
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// Fix: assert against a literal AND add negative cases
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_ValidInput_ReturnsInput()
{
    var result = Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAtSign_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("invalid"));
}
```

---

#### 5. `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 45) — Self-referential round-trip on a lossy mapping

The production `Mapper` is **lossy**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`; `ToEntity` hardcodes `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`. A round-trip assertion on a lossy mapping is fundamentally wrong — it either (a) silently fails (the asserts on `Id` and `Email` should fail: `1 != 0`, `"a@b.com" != ""`), or (b) if it somehow passes, masks data loss. Either way, this test gives false confidence that the mapping preserves data.

```csharp
// Fix: test each mapping direction independently with literal expectations
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age); // Age has no source — document this
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);    // Id is not mapped
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // Email is not mapped
}
```

---

### Medium Findings

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Details |
|---|------|-------------|---------|
| 6 | `Clone_ProducesEqualObject` (line 28) | **Missing reference-inequality check** | Verifies field equality but never asserts `clone != original` (reference). If `Clone()` returned `this`, the test would still pass. Add: `Assert.AreNotSame(original, clone);` |

